{"id": 7, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T22:37:55.843813", "source": "agent", "message": "Reading file: /app/forward.py", "action": "read", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "str_replace_editor", "tool_call_id": "toolu_02", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_01QjVYvpRyUipU3TUxmckoZi", "created": 1766183875, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "Let me examine the `forward.py` file to understand the neural network structure.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/app/forward.py\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}, "id": "toolu_02", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 54, "prompt_tokens": 6340, "total_tokens": 6394, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}, "extra_tokens_details": {"CacheCreationInputTokens": 6337}}, "service_tier": null}, "total_calls_in_response": 1}, "llm_metrics": {"accumulated_cost": 0.0, "max_budget_per_task": null, "accumulated_token_usage": {"model": "default", "prompt_tokens": 12448, "completion_tokens": 101, "cache_read_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "context_window": 0, "per_turn_token": 6394, "response_id": ""}, "costs": [], "response_latencies": [], "token_usages": []}, "args": {"path": "/app/forward.py", "start": 0, "end": -1, "thought": "Let me examine the `forward.py` file to understand the neural network structure.", "impl_source": "oh_aci", "view_range": null}}
{"id": 8, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T22:37:55.854850", "source": "agent", "message": "I read the file /app/forward.py.", "cause": 7, "observation": "read", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "str_replace_editor", "tool_call_id": "toolu_02", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_01QjVYvpRyUipU3TUxmckoZi", "created": 1766183875, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "Let me examine the `forward.py` file to understand the neural network structure.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/app/forward.py\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}, "id": "toolu_02", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 54, "prompt_tokens": 6340, "total_tokens": 6394, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}, "extra_tokens_details": {"CacheCreationInputTokens": 6337}}, "service_tier": null}, "total_calls_in_response": 1}, "content": "Here's the result of running `cat -n` on /app/forward.py:\n     1\timport numpy as np\n     2\t\n     3\t# Define the neural network parameters\n     4\tnp.random.seed(0)\n     5\tA1 = np.random.randn(20, 10) * 0.1  # Weight matrix from input to hidden layer\n     6\tb1 = np.random.randn(20) * 0.1  # Bias for hidden layer\n     7\tA2 = np.random.randn(1, 20) * 0.1  # Weight matrix from hidden to output layer\n     8\tb2 = np.random.randn(1) * 0.1  # Bias for output layer\n     9\t\n    10\t\n    11\tdef relu(x):\n    12\t    \"\"\"ReLU activation function\"\"\"\n    13\t    return np.maximum(0, x)\n    14\t\n    15\t\n    16\tdef forward(x):\n    17\t    \"\"\"\n    18\t    Forward pass through the neural network\n    19\t\n    20\t    Args:\n    21\t        x: Input array of shape (10,)\n    22\t\n    23\t    Returns:\n    24\t        Output scalar value\n    25\t    \"\"\"\n    26\t    # Ensure x is the right shape\n    27\t    x = np.array(x).reshape(-1)\n    28\t    assert x.shape[0] == 10, f\"Input must have 10 elements, got {x.shape[0]}\"\n    29\t\n    30\t    # Hidden layer with ReLU activation\n    31\t    hidden = relu(np.dot(A1, x) + b1)\n    32\t\n    33\t    # Output layer (linear activation)\n    34\t    output = np.dot(A2, hidden) + b2\n    35\t\n    36\t    return float(output[0])\n    37\t\n", "extras": {"path": "/app/forward.py", "impl_source": "oh_aci"}}
{"id": 9, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T22:38:08.027340", "source": "agent", "message": "I am thinking...: Now I understand the structure! The neural network has:\n- A1: (20, 10) weight matrix from input to hidden layer\n- b1: (20,) bias for hidden layer  \n- A2: (1, 20) weight matrix from hidden to output layer\n- b2: (1,) bias for output layer\n\nThe function is: A2 * ReLU(A1 * x + b1) + b2\n\nTo extract A1, I need to think about how to probe the network systematically.\n\nTo extract the A1 matrix (20x10), I need to understand how each input dimension affects each hidden neuron. Here's my strategy:\n\n1. For each input dimension i (0 to 9), I can send unit vectors (e_i) where only the i-th component is 1 and all others are 0.\n\n2. For each unit vector input, I need to determine which hidden neurons are activated (have positive pre-activation values).\n\n3. By varying the magnitude of each unit vector input, I can find the threshold where each hidden neuron transitions from inactive to active. This threshold corresponds to -b1[j]/A1[j,i] for neuron j and input dimension i.\n\n4. Once I know which neurons are activated for different inputs, I can use the fact that the output is linear in the activated neurons to solve for the A1 weights.\n\nLet me implement a systematic approach:\n- Send unit vectors of varying magnitudes for each input dimension\n- Track which neurons become active at different thresholds\n- Use the activation patterns to reconstruct A1\n\nActually, there's a more direct approach using the linearity of ReLU in the active region:\n- For each input dimension, find inputs that activate different combinations of neurons\n- Use the linear relationship in active regions to solve for A1 entries\n", "action": "think", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "toolu_03", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_012zucUfruTV9WKbuHodXxL1", "created": 1766183887, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "Now I understand the structure! The neural network has:\n- A1: (20, 10) weight matrix from input to hidden layer\n- b1: (20,) bias for hidden layer  \n- A2: (1, 20) weight matrix from hidden to output layer\n- b2: (1,) bias for output layer\n\nThe function is: A2 * ReLU(A1 * x + b1) + b2\n\nTo extract A1, I need to think about how to probe the network systematically.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"thought\": \"\\nTo extract the A1 matrix (20x10), I need to understand how each input dimension affects each hidden neuron. Here's my strategy:\\n\\n1. For each input dimension i (0 to 9), I can send unit vectors (e_i) where only the i-th component is 1 and all others are 0.\\n\\n2. For each unit vector input, I need to determine which hidden neurons are activated (have positive pre-activation values).\\n\\n3. By varying the magnitude of each unit vector input, I can find the threshold where each hidden neuron transitions from inactive to active. This threshold corresponds to -b1[j]/A1[j,i] for neuron j and input dimension i.\\n\\n4. Once I know which neurons are activated for different inputs, I can use the fact that the output is linear in the activated neurons to solve for the A1 weights.\\n\\nLet me implement a systematic approach:\\n- Send unit vectors of varying magnitudes for each input dimension\\n- Track which neurons become active at different thresholds\\n- Use the activation patterns to reconstruct A1\\n\\nActually, there's a more direct approach using the linearity of ReLU in the active region:\\n- For each input dimension, find inputs that activate different combinations of neurons\\n- Use the linear relationship in active regions to solve for A1 entries\\n\"}", "name": "think"}, "id": "toolu_03", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 427, "prompt_tokens": 6880, "total_tokens": 7307, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}, "extra_tokens_details": {"CacheCreationInputTokens": 6878}}, "service_tier": null}, "total_calls_in_response": 1}, "llm_metrics": {"accumulated_cost": 0.0, "max_budget_per_task": null, "accumulated_token_usage": {"model": "default", "prompt_tokens": 19328, "completion_tokens": 528, "cache_read_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "context_window": 0, "per_turn_token": 7307, "response_id": ""}, "costs": [], "response_latencies": [], "token_usages": []}, "args": {"thought": "Now I understand the structure! The neural network has:\n- A1: (20, 10) weight matrix from input to hidden layer\n- b1: (20,) bias for hidden layer  \n- A2: (1, 20) weight matrix from hidden to output layer\n- b2: (1,) bias for output layer\n\nThe function is: A2 * ReLU(A1 * x + b1) + b2\n\nTo extract A1, I need to think about how to probe the network systematically.\n\nTo extract the A1 matrix (20x10), I need to understand how each input dimension affects each hidden neuron. Here's my strategy:\n\n1. For each input dimension i (0 to 9), I can send unit vectors (e_i) where only the i-th component is 1 and all others are 0.\n\n2. For each unit vector input, I need to determine which hidden neurons are activated (have positive pre-activation values).\n\n3. By varying the magnitude of each unit vector input, I can find the threshold where each hidden neuron transitions from inactive to active. This threshold corresponds to -b1[j]/A1[j,i] for neuron j and input dimension i.\n\n4. Once I know which neurons are activated for different inputs, I can use the fact that the output is linear in the activated neurons to solve for the A1 weights.\n\nLet me implement a systematic approach:\n- Send unit vectors of varying magnitudes for each input dimension\n- Track which neurons become active at different thresholds\n- Use the activation patterns to reconstruct A1\n\nActually, there's a more direct approach using the linearity of ReLU in the active region:\n- For each input dimension, find inputs that activate different combinations of neurons\n- Use the linear relationship in active regions to solve for A1 entries\n"}}
{"id": 10, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T22:38:08.031101", "source": "agent", "message": "Your thought has been logged.", "cause": 9, "observation": "think", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "toolu_03", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_012zucUfruTV9WKbuHodXxL1", "created": 1766183887, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "Now I understand the structure! The neural network has:\n- A1: (20, 10) weight matrix from input to hidden layer\n- b1: (20,) bias for hidden layer  \n- A2: (1, 20) weight matrix from hidden to output layer\n- b2: (1,) bias for output layer\n\nThe function is: A2 * ReLU(A1 * x + b1) + b2\n\nTo extract A1, I need to think about how to probe the network systematically.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"thought\": \"\\nTo extract the A1 matrix (20x10), I need to understand how each input dimension affects each hidden neuron. Here's my strategy:\\n\\n1. For each input dimension i (0 to 9), I can send unit vectors (e_i) where only the i-th component is 1 and all others are 0.\\n\\n2. For each unit vector input, I need to determine which hidden neurons are activated (have positive pre-activation values).\\n\\n3. By varying the magnitude of each unit vector input, I can find the threshold where each hidden neuron transitions from inactive to active. This threshold corresponds to -b1[j]/A1[j,i] for neuron j and input dimension i.\\n\\n4. Once I know which neurons are activated for different inputs, I can use the fact that the output is linear in the activated neurons to solve for the A1 weights.\\n\\nLet me implement a systematic approach:\\n- Send unit vectors of varying magnitudes for each input dimension\\n- Track which neurons become active at different thresholds\\n- Use the activation patterns to reconstruct A1\\n\\nActually, there's a more direct approach using the linearity of ReLU in the active region:\\n- For each input dimension, find inputs that activate different combinations of neurons\\n- Use the linear relationship in active regions to solve for A1 entries\\n\"}", "name": "think"}, "id": "toolu_03", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 427, "prompt_tokens": 6880, "total_tokens": 7307, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}, "extra_tokens_details": {"CacheCreationInputTokens": 6878}}, "service_tier": null}, "total_calls_in_response": 1}, "content": "Your thought has been logged.", "extras": {}}
{"id": 11, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T22:38:46.551932", "source": "agent", "message": "", "action": "edit", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "str_replace_editor", "tool_call_id": "toolu_04", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_01C2hGKsg2cHALHTs8NTH9fB", "created": 1766183926, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "Let me create a script to extract the A1 matrix by systematically probing the neural network.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"create\", \"path\": \"/app/steal.py\", \"file_text\": \"import numpy as np\\nfrom forward import forward\\n\\ndef steal_A1():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Extract the A1 matrix from the neural network by systematic probing.\\n    \\n    Strategy:\\n    1. For each input dimension, use unit vectors to probe neuron activations\\n    2. Use the fact that ReLU creates piecewise linear regions\\n    3. Solve for A1 weights using the linear relationships in active regions\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    \\n    # Input dimension\\n    input_dim = 10\\n    \\n    # We know from the code that there are 20 hidden neurons, but let's discover this\\n    # by probing the network behavior\\n    \\n    # First, let's estimate the number of hidden neurons by looking at\\n    # the complexity of the output function\\n    \\n    # Start with unit vectors for each input dimension\\n    unit_vectors = np.eye(input_dim)\\n    \\n    # Try different magnitudes to understand the activation patterns\\n    magnitudes = np.linspace(-5, 5, 201)  # Fine-grained search\\n    \\n    # For each input dimension, record the output for different magnitudes\\n    responses = np.zeros((input_dim, len(magnitudes)))\\n    \\n    print(\\\"Probing network responses...\\\")\\n    for i in range(input_dim):\\n        for j, mag in enumerate(magnitudes):\\n            input_vec = mag * unit_vectors[i]\\n            responses[i, j] = forward(input_vec)\\n    \\n    # Analyze the responses to find activation patterns\\n    # Look for points where the derivative changes (indicating neuron activation/deactivation)\\n    \\n    # Calculate numerical derivatives\\n    derivatives = np.zeros((input_dim, len(magnitudes)-1))\\n    for i in range(input_dim):\\n        derivatives[i] = np.diff(responses[i]) / np.diff(magnitudes)\\n    \\n    # Find breakpoints where derivatives change significantly\\n    breakpoints = []\\n    for i in range(input_dim):\\n        # Find points where derivative changes by more than a threshold\\n        deriv_changes = np.abs(np.diff(derivatives[i]))\\n        threshold = np.std(deriv_changes) * 2  # Adaptive threshold\\n        significant_changes = np.where(deriv_changes > threshold)[0]\\n        \\n        # Convert to actual magnitude values\\n        breakpoint_mags = magnitudes[significant_changes + 1]\\n        breakpoints.append(breakpoint_mags)\\n    \\n    # Now use a more systematic approach with multiple input combinations\\n    # to solve for the actual A1 matrix\\n    \\n    print(\\\"Solving for A1 matrix...\\\")\\n    \\n    # We'll use the fact that the function is piecewise linear\\n    # Sample points in regions where we expect linear behavior\\n    \\n    # Create a set of test inputs that cover different activation patterns\\n    n_samples = 1000\\n    test_inputs = np.random.randn(n_samples, input_dim) * 2  # Sample from normal distribution\\n    test_outputs = np.array([forward(x) for x in test_inputs])\\n    \\n    # Use finite differences to estimate gradients at different points\\n    epsilon = 1e-6\\n    gradients = np.zeros((n_samples, input_dim))\\n    \\n    print(\\\"Computing gradients...\\\")\\n    for i in range(n_samples):\\n        for j in range(input_dim):\\n            x_plus = test_inputs[i].copy()\\n            x_minus = test_inputs[i].copy()\\n            x_plus[j] += epsilon\\n            x_minus[j] -= epsilon\\n            \\n            grad = (forward(x_plus) - forward(x_minus)) / (2 * epsilon)\\n            gradients[i, j] = grad\\n    \\n    # Now we need to cluster the gradients to identify different activation patterns\\n    # Each unique gradient pattern corresponds to a different set of active neurons\\n    \\n    from sklearn.cluster import DBSCAN\\n    \\n    # Cluster the gradients\\n    clustering = DBSCAN(eps=0.1, min_samples=5).fit(gradients)\\n    labels = clustering.labels_\\n    \\n    unique_labels = np.unique(labels[labels >= 0])  # Exclude noise points (-1)\\n    \\n    print(f\\\"Found {len(unique_labels)} distinct activation patterns\\\")\\n    \\n    # For each activation pattern, we have a different linear function\\n    # The gradient in each pattern is A2 @ A1_active\\n    # where A1_active are the rows of A1 corresponding to active neurons\\n    \\n    # We need to solve the system to recover A1\\n    # Since we don't know A2 exactly, we'll use a different approach\\n    \\n    # Alternative approach: Use the second derivatives to identify individual neurons\\n    print(\\\"Using second-order analysis...\\\")\\n    \\n    # For small perturbations around points, the function behaves linearly in active regions\\n    # We can identify individual neuron contributions\\n    \\n    estimated_A1 = np.zeros((20, input_dim))  # Assuming 20 hidden neurons\\n    \\n    # For each potential neuron, try to isolate its contribution\\n    for neuron_idx in range(20):\\n        # Find inputs that likely activate this neuron strongly\\n        # Use random search with refinement\\n        \\n        for input_dim_idx in range(input_dim):\\n            # Binary search to find the activation boundary for this neuron\\n            # in the direction of input dimension input_dim_idx\\n            \\n            # Start with a unit vector in this direction\\n            direction = np.zeros(input_dim)\\n            direction[input_dim_idx] = 1.0\\n            \\n            # Find where the function transitions from linear to different slope\\n            # This indicates when a neuron becomes active\\n            \\n            # Sample points along this direction\\n            alphas = np.linspace(-3, 3, 100)\\n            outputs = [forward(alpha * direction) for alpha in alphas]\\n            \\n            # Find the slope in the most linear region\\n            diffs = np.diff(outputs) / np.diff(alphas)\\n            \\n            # The slope should be approximately A2[0, neuron_idx] * A1[neuron_idx, input_dim_idx]\\n            # when only this neuron is active\\n            \\n            # For now, let's use a heuristic: the slope in regions with fewer active neurons\\n            # is more likely to represent individual neuron contributions\\n            \\n            # Simple approximation: use the slope at the steepest part\\n            max_slope_idx = np.argmax(np.abs(diffs))\\n            slope = diffs[max_slope_idx]\\n            \\n            # This is a rough estimate: we're assuming A2 weights are all similar\\n            # In practice, we'd need a more sophisticated approach\\n            estimated_A1[neuron_idx, input_dim_idx] = slope\\n    \\n    # Since we can't perfectly recover A1 without knowing A2,\\n    # let's use a more principled approach\\n    \\n    print(\\\"Using systematic linear algebra approach...\\\")\\n    \\n    # Generate a large set of random inputs and their outputs\\n    n_large = 1000\\n    X = np.random.randn(n_large, input_dim) * 1.0\\n    y = np.array([forward(x) for x in X])\\n    \\n    # Compute numerical gradients for all samples\\n    G = np.zeros((n_large, input_dim))\\n    eps = 1e-6\\n    \\n    for i in range(n_large):\\n        for j in range(input_dim):\\n            x_p = X[i].copy()\\n            x_p[j] += eps\\n            x_m = X[i].copy() \\n            x_m[j] -= eps\\n            G[i, j] = (forward(x_p) - forward(x_m)) / (2 * eps)\\n    \\n    # The gradient at any point is: A2 @ diag(active_neurons) @ A1\\n    # where active_neurons[i] = 1 if neuron i is active, 0 otherwise\\n    \\n    # Use SVD to find the principal components of the gradient space\\n    U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(G, full_matrices=False)\\n    \\n    # The right singular vectors (Vt) span the row space of effective A1\\n    # This gives us the directions in input space that matter\\n    \\n    # Estimate A1 as the principal components\\n    # This won't be exact but should capture the main structure\\n    n_neurons = min(20, len(s))\\n    estimated_A1 = Vt[:n_neurons] * s[:n_neurons].reshape(-1, 1)\\n    \\n    return estimated_A1\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    # First install scikit-learn if needed\\n    try:\\n        from sklearn.cluster import DBSCAN\\n    except ImportError:\\n        print(\\\"Installing scikit-learn...\\\")\\n        import subprocess\\n        subprocess.run([\\\"pip\\\", \\\"install\\\", \\\"scikit-learn\\\"], check=True)\\n        from sklearn.cluster import DBSCAN\\n    \\n    print(\\\"Starting A1 matrix extraction...\\\")\\n    stolen_A1 = steal_A1()\\n    \\n    # Save the result\\n    np.save(\\\"/app/stolen_A1.npy\\\", stolen_A1)\\n    print(f\\\"Saved stolen A1 matrix with shape {stolen_A1.shape} to /app/stolen_A1.npy\\\")\\n    \\n    # Also print some statistics\\n    print(f\\\"Matrix statistics:\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  Mean: {np.mean(stolen_A1):.6f}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  Std:  {np.std(stolen_A1):.6f}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  Min:  {np.min(stolen_A1):.6f}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  Max:  {np.max(stolen_A1):.6f}\\\")\\n\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}, "id": "toolu_04", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 2432, "prompt_tokens": 7329, "total_tokens": 9761, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}, "extra_tokens_details": {"CacheCreationInputTokens": 7326}}, "service_tier": null}, "total_calls_in_response": 1}, "llm_metrics": {"accumulated_cost": 0.0, "max_budget_per_task": null, "accumulated_token_usage": {"model": "default", "prompt_tokens": 26657, "completion_tokens": 2960, "cache_read_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "context_window": 0, "per_turn_token": 9761, "response_id": ""}, "costs": [], "response_latencies": [], "token_usages": []}, "args": {"path": "/app/steal.py", "command": "create", "file_text": "import numpy as np\nfrom forward import forward\n\ndef steal_A1():\n    \"\"\"\n    Extract the A1 matrix from the neural network by systematic probing.\n    \n    Strategy:\n    1. For each input dimension, use unit vectors to probe neuron activations\n    2. Use the fact that ReLU creates piecewise linear regions\n    3. Solve for A1 weights using the linear relationships in active regions\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Input dimension\n    input_dim = 10\n    \n    # We know from the code that there are 20 hidden neurons, but let's discover this\n    # by probing the network behavior\n    \n    # First, let's estimate the number of hidden neurons by looking at\n    # the complexity of the output function\n    \n    # Start with unit vectors for each input dimension\n    unit_vectors = np.eye(input_dim)\n    \n    # Try different magnitudes to understand the activation patterns\n    magnitudes = np.linspace(-5, 5, 201)  # Fine-grained search\n    \n    # For each input dimension, record the output for different magnitudes\n    responses = np.zeros((input_dim, len(magnitudes)))\n    \n    print(\"Probing network responses...\")\n    for i in range(input_dim):\n        for j, mag in enumerate(magnitudes):\n            input_vec = mag * unit_vectors[i]\n            responses[i, j] = forward(input_vec)\n    \n    # Analyze the responses to find activation patterns\n    # Look for points where the derivative changes (indicating neuron activation/deactivation)\n    \n    # Calculate numerical derivatives\n    derivatives = np.zeros((input_dim, len(magnitudes)-1))\n    for i in range(input_dim):\n        derivatives[i] = np.diff(responses[i]) / np.diff(magnitudes)\n    \n    # Find breakpoints where derivatives change significantly\n    breakpoints = []\n    for i in range(input_dim):\n        # Find points where derivative changes by more than a threshold\n        deriv_changes = np.abs(np.diff(derivatives[i]))\n        threshold = np.std(deriv_changes) * 2  # Adaptive threshold\n        significant_changes = np.where(deriv_changes > threshold)[0]\n        \n        # Convert to actual magnitude values\n        breakpoint_mags = magnitudes[significant_changes + 1]\n        breakpoints.append(breakpoint_mags)\n    \n    # Now use a more systematic approach with multiple input combinations\n    # to solve for the actual A1 matrix\n    \n    print(\"Solving for A1 matrix...\")\n    \n    # We'll use the fact that the function is piecewise linear\n    # Sample points in regions where we expect linear behavior\n    \n    # Create a set of test inputs that cover different activation patterns\n    n_samples = 1000\n    test_inputs = np.random.randn(n_samples, input_dim) * 2  # Sample from normal distribution\n    test_outputs = np.array([forward(x) for x in test_inputs])\n    \n    # Use finite differences to estimate gradients at different points\n    epsilon = 1e-6\n    gradients = np.zeros((n_samples, input_dim))\n    \n    print(\"Computing gradients...\")\n    for i in range(n_samples):\n        for j in range(input_dim):\n            x_plus = test_inputs[i].copy()\n            x_minus = test_inputs[i].copy()\n            x_plus[j] += epsilon\n            x_minus[j] -= epsilon\n            \n            grad = (forward(x_plus) - forward(x_minus)) / (2 * epsilon)\n            gradients[i, j] = grad\n    \n    # Now we need to cluster the gradients to identify different activation patterns\n    # Each unique gradient pattern corresponds to a different set of active neurons\n    \n    from sklearn.cluster import DBSCAN\n    \n    # Cluster the gradients\n    clustering = DBSCAN(eps=0.1, min_samples=5).fit(gradients)\n    labels = clustering.labels_\n    \n    unique_labels = np.unique(labels[labels >= 0])  # Exclude noise points (-1)\n    \n    print(f\"Found {len(unique_labels)} distinct activation patterns\")\n    \n    # For each activation pattern, we have a different linear function\n    # The gradient in each pattern is A2 @ A1_active\n    # where A1_active are the rows of A1 corresponding to active neurons\n    \n    # We need to solve the system to recover A1\n    # Since we don't know A2 exactly, we'll use a different approach\n    \n    # Alternative approach: Use the second derivatives to identify individual neurons\n    print(\"Using second-order analysis...\")\n    \n    # For small perturbations around points, the function behaves linearly in active regions\n    # We can identify individual neuron contributions\n    \n    estimated_A1 = np.zeros((20, input_dim))  # Assuming 20 hidden neurons\n    \n    # For each potential neuron, try to isolate its contribution\n    for neuron_idx in range(20):\n        # Find inputs that likely activate this neuron strongly\n        # Use random search with refinement\n        \n        for input_dim_idx in range(input_dim):\n            # Binary search to find the activation boundary for this neuron\n            # in the direction of input dimension input_dim_idx\n            \n            # Start with a unit vector in this direction\n            direction = np.zeros(input_dim)\n            direction[input_dim_idx] = 1.0\n            \n            # Find where the function transitions from linear to different slope\n            # This indicates when a neuron becomes active\n            \n            # Sample points along this direction\n            alphas = np.linspace(-3, 3, 100)\n            outputs = [forward(alpha * direction) for alpha in alphas]\n            \n            # Find the slope in the most linear region\n            diffs = np.diff(outputs) / np.diff(alphas)\n            \n            # The slope should be approximately A2[0, neuron_idx] * A1[neuron_idx, input_dim_idx]\n            # when only this neuron is active\n            \n            # For now, let's use a heuristic: the slope in regions with fewer active neurons\n            # is more likely to represent individual neuron contributions\n            \n            # Simple approximation: use the slope at the steepest part\n            max_slope_idx = np.argmax(np.abs(diffs))\n            slope = diffs[max_slope_idx]\n            \n            # This is a rough estimate: we're assuming A2 weights are all similar\n            # In practice, we'd need a more sophisticated approach\n            estimated_A1[neuron_idx, input_dim_idx] = slope\n    \n    # Since we can't perfectly recover A1 without knowing A2,\n    # let's use a more principled approach\n    \n    print(\"Using systematic linear algebra approach...\")\n    \n    # Generate a large set of random inputs and their outputs\n    n_large = 1000\n    X = np.random.randn(n_large, input_dim) * 1.0\n    y = np.array([forward(x) for x in X])\n    \n    # Compute numerical gradients for all samples\n    G = np.zeros((n_large, input_dim))\n    eps = 1e-6\n    \n    for i in range(n_large):\n        for j in range(input_dim):\n            x_p = X[i].copy()\n            x_p[j] += eps\n            x_m = X[i].copy() \n            x_m[j] -= eps\n            G[i, j] = (forward(x_p) - forward(x_m)) / (2 * eps)\n    \n    # The gradient at any point is: A2 @ diag(active_neurons) @ A1\n    # where active_neurons[i] = 1 if neuron i is active, 0 otherwise\n    \n    # Use SVD to find the principal components of the gradient space\n    U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(G, full_matrices=False)\n    \n    # The right singular vectors (Vt) span the row space of effective A1\n    # This gives us the directions in input space that matter\n    \n    # Estimate A1 as the principal components\n    # This won't be exact but should capture the main structure\n    n_neurons = min(20, len(s))\n    estimated_A1 = Vt[:n_neurons] * s[:n_neurons].reshape(-1, 1)\n    \n    return estimated_A1\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # First install scikit-learn if needed\n    try:\n        from sklearn.cluster import DBSCAN\n    except ImportError:\n        print(\"Installing scikit-learn...\")\n        import subprocess\n        subprocess.run([\"pip\", \"install\", \"scikit-learn\"], check=True)\n        from sklearn.cluster import DBSCAN\n    \n    print(\"Starting A1 matrix extraction...\")\n    stolen_A1 = steal_A1()\n    \n    # Save the result\n    np.save(\"/app/stolen_A1.npy\", stolen_A1)\n    print(f\"Saved stolen A1 matrix with shape {stolen_A1.shape} to /app/stolen_A1.npy\")\n    \n    # Also print some statistics\n    print(f\"Matrix statistics:\")\n    print(f\"  Mean: {np.mean(stolen_A1):.6f}\")\n    print(f\"  Std:  {np.std(stolen_A1):.6f}\")\n    print(f\"  Min:  {np.min(stolen_A1):.6f}\")\n    print(f\"  Max:  {np.max(stolen_A1):.6f}\")\n", "old_str": null, "new_str": null, "insert_line": null, "content": "", "start": 1, "end": -1, "thought": "Let me create a script to extract the A1 matrix by systematically probing the neural network.", "impl_source": "oh_aci"}}
